{
  "dom_id": "step-q107144",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $B={f\\in C[0,1]\\,|\\,\\,f(x)\\le 1\\,\\,\u2200x\\in [0,1]}.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $B$ is closed under function addition\n- $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $B$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n- $B$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if  $f(x)=g(x)=1$ for all $x\\in [0,1]$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(x) & =f(x)+g(x) \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in [0,1].$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example,  $f(x)=1$ for all $x\\in [0,1]$ and take and $k=2,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(kf)(x) & =2f(x) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in [0,1].$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $B$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"",
